WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.310
Here's a question a bank faces all the time. It has twenty

00:03.410 --> 00:07.191
thousand customers and a new credit card. What share of them would

00:07.291 --> 00:11.072
sign up? Asking everyone is slow and expensive, so instead it asks

00:11.172 --> 00:13.953
a few hundred, and hopes the answer carries over.

00:15.050 --> 00:17.805
Statistics is the science of when that hope is

00:17.905 --> 00:20.226
justified, and how far we can trust it.

00:21.316 --> 00:24.375
Picture the whole population of customers. Each dot is

00:24.475 --> 00:26.832
a customer; the yellow ones would sign up.

00:26.916 --> 00:31.211
The share of yellow dots is one fixed number, here thirty percent.

00:31.311 --> 00:35.672
A number that describes the whole population is called a parameter.

00:35.766 --> 00:38.709
The trouble is that in real life we can't see the

00:38.809 --> 00:42.249
colours. The parameter is there, but it's hidden from us.

00:42.333 --> 00:45.838
So we pick fifty customers at random, and look only at them.

00:45.933 --> 00:48.808
Seventeen of them say yes: thirty-four percent. A

00:48.908 --> 00:51.418
number computed from the sample is called a

00:51.518 --> 00:54.636
statistic. It's our window onto the hidden parameter.

00:55.733 --> 00:59.037
Now draw another fifty. You get a different answer. And

00:59.137 --> 01:03.183
another, and another. Each sample tells a slightly different story.

01:03.283 --> 01:06.030
The statistic is random. The parameter is not.

01:06.116 --> 01:09.090
If we repeated this two hundred times, the answers

01:09.190 --> 01:12.103
would pile up around one value, with some spread.

01:12.200 --> 01:15.134
And the value they pile up around is exactly the

01:15.234 --> 01:19.054
hidden parameter, thirty percent. A single sample may miss it,

01:19.154 --> 01:22.026
but the statistic is aimed at the right target.

01:23.116 --> 01:26.297
Now take bigger samples. Here are a hundred and fifty samples

01:26.397 --> 01:29.416
of fifty, and a hundred and fifty samples of five hundred.

01:29.516 --> 01:33.704
The pile gets much narrower. The spread shrinks roughly like one over

01:33.804 --> 01:37.308
the square root of the sample size, so four times the data

01:37.408 --> 01:41.968
buys you half the uncertainty. That is the heart of inferential statistics.

01:42.068 --> 01:45.821
A sample can't tell you the parameter exactly, but it can tell

01:45.921 --> 01:48.120
you how far off you are likely to be.

01:49.216 --> 01:52.030
One more idea, which sets up the rest of the course.

01:52.130 --> 01:55.505
Count how often something happens, say a stock closing up, and

01:55.605 --> 01:58.755
divide by the number of days. That's a relative frequency.

01:58.850 --> 02:02.619
Track it as the days pile up. At first it jumps around wildly.

02:02.719 --> 02:07.112
Then it wobbles less and less, and settles near one value. That settling

02:07.212 --> 02:11.105
value is what we will call a probability, starting next lecture.

02:12.200 --> 02:17.280
So, two pairs of words to keep. A population has parameters: fixed, but

02:17.380 --> 02:22.972
hidden. A sample has statistics: random, but visible. Statistics is the art of

02:23.072 --> 02:27.277
reasoning from the part you can see to the whole you can't.
